WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:05.800
MobileNet V1 and V2 gave you

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.050
a way to implement
a neural network,

3
00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:10.570
that is more
computationally efficient.

4
00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:13.315
But is there a way
to tune MobileNet,

5
00:00:13.315 --> 00:00:15.070
or some other architecture,

6
00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.240
to your specific device?

7
00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.340
Maybe you're implementing a
computer vision algorithm for

8
00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.275
different brands of
mobile phones with

9
00:00:23.275 --> 00:00:25.450
different amounts of
compute resources,

10
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:27.355
or for different edge devices.

11
00:00:27.355 --> 00:00:29.485
If you have a little
bit more computation,

12
00:00:29.485 --> 00:00:31.720
maybe you have a slightly
bigger neural network

13
00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.910
and hopefully you get
a bit more accuracy,

14
00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:36.865
or if you are more
computationally constraint,

15
00:00:36.865 --> 00:00:39.250
maybe you want a slightly
smaller neural network

16
00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:40.625
that runs a bit faster,

17
00:00:40.625 --> 00:00:42.915
at the cost of a little
bit of accuracy.

18
00:00:42.915 --> 00:00:45.440
How can you automatically
scale up or

19
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.470
down neural networks for
a particular device?

20
00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:51.595
EfficientNet, gives
you a way to do so.

21
00:00:51.595 --> 00:00:53.135
Let's say you have

22
00:00:53.135 --> 00:00:56.300
a baseline neural
network architecture,

23
00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.765
where the input image has
a certain resolution r,

24
00:00:59.765 --> 00:01:03.305
and your new network
has a certain depth,

25
00:01:03.305 --> 00:01:07.770
and the layers has
a certain width.

26
00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.290
The authors of the
EfficientNet paper,

27
00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:13.885
Mingxing Tan and my former
PhD student, Quoc Le,

28
00:01:13.885 --> 00:01:16.100
observed that the
three things you

29
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:18.640
could do to scale
things up or down,

30
00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.890
are, you could use a
high resolution image.

31
00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:24.425
So a new image resolution

32
00:01:24.425 --> 00:01:27.890
r. I don't know

33
00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.860
how to denote a high resolution
in a video like this.

34
00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:33.620
I'm using this blue
glow here to denote,

35
00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.585
maybe high resolution image.

36
00:01:35.585 --> 00:01:39.595
Or you could make this
network much deeper.

37
00:01:39.595 --> 00:01:45.450
You could vary d to depth
of the neural network,

38
00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:49.000
or you can make the layers wider.

39
00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:55.070
You can also vary the
width of these layers.

40
00:01:55.070 --> 00:02:00.455
The question is, given a
particular computational budget,

41
00:02:00.455 --> 00:02:04.530
what's the good choice
of r, d, and w?

42
00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:08.150
Or depending on the computational
resources you have,

43
00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.684
you can also use
compound scaling,

44
00:02:10.684 --> 00:02:13.370
where you might
simultaneously scale up or

45
00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:17.265
simultaneously scale down
the resolution of the image,

46
00:02:17.265 --> 00:02:19.865
and the depth, and the width
of the neural network.

47
00:02:19.865 --> 00:02:21.725
Now the tricky part is,

48
00:02:21.725 --> 00:02:24.630
if you want to scale up r, d,

49
00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:26.160
and w, what's the rate at

50
00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.020
which you should scale
up each of these?

51
00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:29.600
Should you double the resolution

52
00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:30.965
and leave depth with the same,

53
00:02:30.965 --> 00:02:33.290
or maybe you should
double the depth,

54
00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:34.625
but leave the others the same,

55
00:02:34.625 --> 00:02:36.590
or increase resolution
by 10 percent,

56
00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:37.880
increase depth by 50 percent,

57
00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:39.230
and width by 20 percent?

58
00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:43.100
What's the best trade-off
between r, d, and w,

59
00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:46.205
to scale up or down
your neural network,

60
00:02:46.205 --> 00:02:48.140
to get the best
possible performance

61
00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:50.440
within your computational budget?

62
00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:52.910
If you are ever looking to adapt

63
00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:56.225
a neural network architecture
for a particular device,

64
00:02:56.225 --> 00:02:57.620
look at one of the open

65
00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:59.885
source implementations
of EfficientNet,

66
00:02:59.885 --> 00:03:03.440
which will help you to choose
a good trade-off between r,

67
00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:06.225
d, and w. That's it.

68
00:03:06.225 --> 00:03:08.870
With MobileNet, you've
learned how to build more

69
00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:12.485
computationally efficient
layers, and with EfficientNet,

70
00:03:12.485 --> 00:03:16.475
you can also find a way
to scale up or down

71
00:03:16.475 --> 00:03:18.635
these neural networks based on

72
00:03:18.635 --> 00:03:21.920
the resources of a device
you may be working on.

73
00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.380
With this, I hope
you have the skills

74
00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.410
needed in order to build
neural networks for

75
00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:30.290
mobile devices and for
embedded devices and for

76
00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:32.630
other devices where
the computation

77
00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:34.650
in a memory maybe more limited.

78
00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.290
I hope this will open up a lot of

79
00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:39.840
possible applications that
you may now be able to build.